1
00:00:00,148 --> 00:00:01,940
PETER STONE: Thanks,
Dawn, for inviting me.

2
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,607
and thanks to all of
you for being here.

3
00:00:03,607 --> 00:00:07,760
It's a pleasure
to be joining you.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:12,042
So what I'm going to do in
this session in this talk is,

5
00:00:12,042 --> 00:00:14,500
as advertised by the title,
I'm going to talk about my view

6
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:19,360
of autonomous agents, and
in particular a broad view,

7
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:23,700
not just the latest
incarnation of, quote,

8
00:00:23,700 --> 00:00:27,500
"agentic artificial intelligence
or agentic large language

9
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:32,140
models," but really the roots of
the term autonomous agents with

10
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,600
emphasis on embodiment,
interaction, and learning.

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,760
And what I mean by
embodiment is really robots.

12
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:42,580
I know I looked
through the topics that

13
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:44,400
have been covered so
far this semester,

14
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:46,300
and there's been a
bunch on learning

15
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:49,220
and a bunch on interaction.

16
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:52,320
So you had from Noam Brown, you
heard about multi-agent systems.

17
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:53,780
And also I think
your last session

18
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:58,300
was on multi-agent systems
and also a lot on learning

19
00:00:58,300 --> 00:00:59,800
and scientific discovery.

20
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:02,380
But I think there's been
less coverage so far

21
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:06,460
of the embodiment side, which
sometimes is physical robots,

22
00:01:06,460 --> 00:01:09,690
sometimes simulated robots.

23
00:01:09,690 --> 00:01:12,870
And so I'm going to
place an emphasis there.

24
00:01:12,870 --> 00:01:17,610
I'm going to start by giving
a broad overview of where I'm

25
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:22,410
coming from, the kind of
research I do in my lab,

26
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:27,070
and so tell you about a
lot of things very briefly,

27
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:29,590
so almost nothing about
a whole bunch of things.

28
00:01:29,590 --> 00:01:32,270
But then I'll do a deeper
dive on one or two things,

29
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:34,210
depending on how
much time we have,

30
00:01:34,210 --> 00:01:38,010
that go into more of
the technical details.

31
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:41,033
And of course, there's more
information on my web page

32
00:01:41,033 --> 00:01:42,450
and my papers about
all the things

33
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:44,307
I'm going to talk about here.

34
00:01:44,307 --> 00:01:45,890
And you're always
welcome to reach out

35
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:47,650
if you have questions
about things

36
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:51,930
that I only touch on briefly.

37
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:56,790
So first, I'm going to just
talk about what my view is.

38
00:01:56,790 --> 00:01:59,950
And I'm jumping between a bunch
of different slide decks here.

39
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:02,650
So apologies if it's
not one unified view.

40
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:05,910
Hopefully things will
go smoothly enough.

41
00:02:05,910 --> 00:02:09,230
But my view of agents,
autonomous agents

42
00:02:09,230 --> 00:02:11,870
have been a focus of
study in the field

43
00:02:11,870 --> 00:02:14,816
of artificial intelligence
for a long time,

44
00:02:14,816 --> 00:02:18,390
for at least three decades,
actually, more than that.

45
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:23,190
I've been involved in the
field for around three decades.

46
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:30,190
And so much longer than this
year's buzzword of agentic AI.

47
00:02:30,190 --> 00:02:34,110
But what is-- fundamentally,
what is an agent?

48
00:02:34,110 --> 00:02:38,350
My view is, very simply, an
agent is an intelligent system

49
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:39,830
that interacts with
the environment

50
00:02:39,830 --> 00:02:43,770
through sensors and effectors,
as shown in the diagram here.

51
00:02:43,770 --> 00:02:47,230

52
00:02:47,230 --> 00:02:53,010
I mean, you could ask the
question, is a chatbot an agent?

53
00:02:53,010 --> 00:02:56,790
Usually, we think of an
autonomous agent as one

54
00:02:56,790 --> 00:03:01,570
where there's not necessarily
an input-output sequence

55
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:03,990
and then it sort of
waits for more input,

56
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:07,650
but rather more of a
continuous loop where there's

57
00:03:07,650 --> 00:03:09,610
sensors coming in
from the environment,

58
00:03:09,610 --> 00:03:12,210
or sensations coming in from
the environment, actions

59
00:03:12,210 --> 00:03:14,910
that are taken that
affect the next sensations

60
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:17,570
but you don't have to wait
to be called or invoked.

61
00:03:17,570 --> 00:03:20,890
And so there are
software agents.

62
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:23,250
There are also physical
or embodied agents, which

63
00:03:23,250 --> 00:03:26,310
usually go by the term robot.

64
00:03:26,310 --> 00:03:28,089
The first slide was
a modified slide

65
00:03:28,089 --> 00:03:31,850
from a definition
in a term when I--

66
00:03:31,850 --> 00:03:34,110
or a class when I teach
robotics in the first session,

67
00:03:34,110 --> 00:03:37,210
I usually ask what is a robot.

68
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:38,710
And it's almost the
same definition.

69
00:03:38,710 --> 00:03:40,650
It's a robot is an
intelligent system that

70
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:42,290
interacts with the
physical environment

71
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:43,540
through sensors and effectors.

72
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:46,650
So the only word I've added
here is the word physical.

73
00:03:46,650 --> 00:03:50,550
But otherwise, so all
robots are agents,

74
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,410
not all agents are robots.

75
00:03:53,410 --> 00:03:56,210
And then we often
think about what's

76
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:59,090
an intelligent complete robot,
or you could substitute the word

77
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:02,290
agent here, an
intelligent complete agent

78
00:04:02,290 --> 00:04:06,340
is one that has some cognition,
the intelligence aspect.

79
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:10,440
So it gets the sensations,
it has the actions.

80
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:13,860
And there's the raw sensors
and the raw actuators,

81
00:04:13,860 --> 00:04:15,800
which if it's a
robot, you can think

82
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:19,520
of as being the
cameras and the motors.

83
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:21,940
But then there's the intelligent
part shown in green here,

84
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:25,320
which is the sensing and
modeling the world based

85
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:28,680
on the raw sensor perceptions,
sensor readings, that

86
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:31,440
is the perception module.

87
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:33,420
On the other side,
the far other side,

88
00:04:33,420 --> 00:04:37,800
there's the action
aspect, which is,

89
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:39,500
once you've decided
what you want to do,

90
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,700
how do you actually do it?

91
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:43,580
And then translate that
into the motor commands,

92
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:45,300
the low-level actuators.

93
00:04:45,300 --> 00:04:48,880
And in between is what
many people consider

94
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:51,400
the brains of the
system or the cognition

95
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:53,642
where you're deciding
what actions to take.

96
00:04:53,642 --> 00:04:55,100
And that could be
through planning,

97
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:58,800
through multi-agent
coordination, teamwork,

98
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:02,620
opponent modeling or
teammate modeling, et cetera.

99
00:05:02,620 --> 00:05:08,160
So all of my research takes
this view of what an agent is.

100
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:13,700
And I just wanted to frame that
in the context of this talk

101
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:16,220
because I think--

102
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:18,940
like I said, nowadays,
there's some people take

103
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:21,020
a more narrow view
of what an agent is

104
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:24,480
or an agentic system is, but
really it's quite a broad term

105
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:27,780
and broadly encompassing.

106
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:31,700
So as I said, and I'm going
to start by telling you almost

107
00:05:31,700 --> 00:05:34,340
nothing about a lot of things.

108
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:36,280
And starting from
where I'm coming from,

109
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:38,820
I am here at UT
Austin, where there's

110
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:41,900
a lot of exciting
things happening.

111
00:05:41,900 --> 00:05:46,540
We were awarded one of the first
National Science Foundation AI

112
00:05:46,540 --> 00:05:49,360
institutes, the Institute for
Foundations of Machine Learning.

113
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:52,820
This is an organization that
spawned the Machine Learning

114
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:56,820
Lab that's headed by Adam
Klivans and Sanjay Shakkottai.

115
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:00,395
We also have an ethical
AI system on campus

116
00:06:00,395 --> 00:06:02,520
that's inspired by the One
Hundred Year Study of AI

117
00:06:02,520 --> 00:06:04,320
that I've been a leader in.

118
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:06,740
But the one on campus here
at UT is called Good Systems,

119
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:08,990
and it involves people from
many different disciplines

120
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:11,520
and the ethical AI initiative.

121
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:15,026
I was the founding
director of Texas Robotics.

122
00:06:15,026 --> 00:06:16,960
We have a beautiful new space.

123
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:18,473
We're going to be hosting--

124
00:06:18,473 --> 00:06:20,140
this is before we
filled it with robots,

125
00:06:20,140 --> 00:06:23,560
it's an old gymnasium that's
now filled with robots

126
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:24,453
from across campus--

127
00:06:24,453 --> 00:06:26,120
we're going to be
hosting the conference

128
00:06:26,120 --> 00:06:29,220
on robot learning here in
about a year next November.

129
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:32,000
So I hope many of you will
have a chance to come on campus

130
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,720
and see that.

131
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:36,400
And there's also a lot of
innovative AI education going on

132
00:06:36,400 --> 00:06:37,080
here.

133
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:41,200
We have an online AI master's
program at a price point

134
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:42,340
of $10,000.

135
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:47,840
We have created an AI literacy
course for the entire campus

136
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:50,428
with all of the
materials online.

137
00:06:50,428 --> 00:06:52,720
So these are all things that
I'd be happy to talk about

138
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,545
in much more detail.

139
00:06:54,545 --> 00:06:56,920
But from the perspective of
my own research in the things

140
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:59,070
I'm going to talk
to you about today,

141
00:06:59,070 --> 00:07:03,330
the unifying question
that's held together,

142
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:06,470
all of the different things
that go on in my lab,

143
00:07:06,470 --> 00:07:08,950
and have over the past
several decades, is this,

144
00:07:08,950 --> 00:07:12,030
it's what degree can
autonomous, intelligent agents

145
00:07:12,030 --> 00:07:13,470
learn in the
presence of teammates

146
00:07:13,470 --> 00:07:16,050
and/or adversaries in
real-time dynamic domains?

147
00:07:16,050 --> 00:07:19,450
And I'll emphasize,
again, this question

148
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:24,250
was the core question of
my PhD thesis back in 1998

149
00:07:24,250 --> 00:07:27,250
and has appeared in almost every
talk I've given since then.

150
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:29,570
And it has included
this term agent.

151
00:07:29,570 --> 00:07:32,532
So that's a way of emphasizing
that this is not a new term.

152
00:07:32,532 --> 00:07:33,990
It's not a new
buzzword, but really

153
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:36,670
something that's been
around for a very

154
00:07:36,670 --> 00:07:38,870
long time in the community.

155
00:07:38,870 --> 00:07:42,030
This question causes
us to focus in my group

156
00:07:42,030 --> 00:07:44,630
on publishing in several
different subareas of AI

157
00:07:44,630 --> 00:07:47,830
autonomous agents,
multi-agent systems, robotics,

158
00:07:47,830 --> 00:07:50,250
machine learning, especially
reinforcement learning.

159
00:07:50,250 --> 00:07:53,110
And we do both foundational
bottom-up research

160
00:07:53,110 --> 00:07:54,770
and more use-inspired research.

161
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:57,230
And when I give a
one-slide introduction

162
00:07:57,230 --> 00:07:58,890
to things that go
on in my lab, it's

163
00:07:58,890 --> 00:08:01,690
a lot easier to talk about those
use-inspired or the use cases

164
00:08:01,690 --> 00:08:02,950
that motivate the research.

165
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:07,090
So one has been that
I focused on a lot

166
00:08:07,090 --> 00:08:09,150
and found inspiring
is robot soccer.

167
00:08:09,150 --> 00:08:12,350
So here's some of the
old Sony AIBO robots.

168
00:08:12,350 --> 00:08:14,930
This is a video from
probably almost 20 years ago

169
00:08:14,930 --> 00:08:18,610
of the robots sensing, deciding,
and acting no remote control

170
00:08:18,610 --> 00:08:23,130
from the RoboCup, or
robot soccer competitions.

171
00:08:23,130 --> 00:08:26,210
We've now moved towards
humanoid robots.

172
00:08:26,210 --> 00:08:29,772
So this is a clip from also a
competition several years ago,

173
00:08:29,772 --> 00:08:32,230
where we were playing a team
from the University of Bremen.

174
00:08:32,230 --> 00:08:35,570
Our robots are the ones with
their hands behind their backs

175
00:08:35,570 --> 00:08:37,690
and pink waistbands.

176
00:08:37,690 --> 00:08:41,030
And again, remember that
they're fully autonomous.

177
00:08:41,030 --> 00:08:43,670
They're sensing, deciding,
and acting like an agent does.

178
00:08:43,670 --> 00:08:45,430
This is a multi-agent system.

179
00:08:45,430 --> 00:08:49,210
So there's several robots
cooperating together.

180
00:08:49,210 --> 00:08:52,690
Here, you see a robot
going in on a breakaway,

181
00:08:52,690 --> 00:08:56,190
and it'll end up scoring a goal.

182
00:08:56,190 --> 00:08:58,670
And we went on to win this game
and winning the competition

183
00:08:58,670 --> 00:09:00,910
that year.

184
00:09:00,910 --> 00:09:04,550
And there's all kinds
of agent challenges

185
00:09:04,550 --> 00:09:07,530
that come up in this
kind of a setting.

186
00:09:07,530 --> 00:09:09,730
The goal of RoboCup, the
Robot Soccer World Cup,

187
00:09:09,730 --> 00:09:11,790
is to create a team
of humanoid robots

188
00:09:11,790 --> 00:09:15,270
that can beat the best World Cup
champion on a real soccer field

189
00:09:15,270 --> 00:09:18,070
by the year 2050.

190
00:09:18,070 --> 00:09:19,450
And so this is an
ambitious goal,

191
00:09:19,450 --> 00:09:23,070
but it's sort of in
that line of AI systems

192
00:09:23,070 --> 00:09:28,110
that have done better than the
best humans at various tasks,

193
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:30,990
from chess to go, to poker.

194
00:09:30,990 --> 00:09:35,950
You heard about, I think,
about StarCraft in this course

195
00:09:35,950 --> 00:09:36,730
already.

196
00:09:36,730 --> 00:09:38,650
And I'll talk about
another one as we go,

197
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:42,768
but soccer would be taking it
to the next level in many ways.

198
00:09:42,768 --> 00:09:44,810
And if you watch that
video, you might be saying,

199
00:09:44,810 --> 00:09:47,870
oh, well, that's not even
close to human level.

200
00:09:47,870 --> 00:09:49,970
The robots are still not
close to human level,

201
00:09:49,970 --> 00:09:51,490
but there's been big leaps.

202
00:09:51,490 --> 00:09:55,670
So that's roughly what it
looked like at the competition

203
00:09:55,670 --> 00:09:56,750
two years ago.

204
00:09:56,750 --> 00:10:00,210
But in this year's competition,
we had a game of humanoid robots

205
00:10:00,210 --> 00:10:02,570
for the first time with people.

206
00:10:02,570 --> 00:10:04,510
And you'll see me
here with the ball.

207
00:10:04,510 --> 00:10:06,950
And I was playing on the
team with the robots,

208
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:09,590
because it's still too easy for
the people to beat the robot.

209
00:10:09,590 --> 00:10:13,270
So it was three people
against me and the robots.

210
00:10:13,270 --> 00:10:15,270
And I had no idea what
was going to happen.

211
00:10:15,270 --> 00:10:17,390
The robots didn't
actually even sense me,

212
00:10:17,390 --> 00:10:19,170
but I left the
ball for this robot

213
00:10:19,170 --> 00:10:21,022
here that I'm showing
with my pointer,

214
00:10:21,022 --> 00:10:22,230
and you'll see what happened.

215
00:10:22,230 --> 00:10:24,490
And this was very
unexpected, but shows

216
00:10:24,490 --> 00:10:27,130
you the level at
which we're getting

217
00:10:27,130 --> 00:10:28,470
to now with robot soccer.

218
00:10:28,470 --> 00:10:30,530
So the robot just turned
around and kicked it

219
00:10:30,530 --> 00:10:34,130
between the RoboCup president's
legs, that's Ubbo Visser.

220
00:10:34,130 --> 00:10:36,590
I tried to get the robot to
give me a hi-five afterwards,

221
00:10:36,590 --> 00:10:37,996
it didn't.

222
00:10:37,996 --> 00:10:41,170
But in any case, we're
at this level where--

223
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:44,650
and we would never have been
able to see that, I think,

224
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:48,650
even a couple years ago, or even
a couple months ago, let me say.

225
00:10:48,650 --> 00:10:51,305
And so there is a
lot of progress.

226
00:10:51,305 --> 00:10:53,680
And I think it's not completely
out of the other question

227
00:10:53,680 --> 00:10:57,360
that by the year 2050, we would
have humanoid robots playing

228
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,000
at human professional level.

229
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,980
I wouldn't bet my life on it.

230
00:11:01,980 --> 00:11:04,228
I think they're
trying to make systems

231
00:11:04,228 --> 00:11:06,520
like that happen in the real
world, in physical systems

232
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:09,160
is much harder than
doing it all in software.

233
00:11:09,160 --> 00:11:12,600
But there's been a lot of
progress in the past 25 years

234
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:14,520
or so.

235
00:11:14,520 --> 00:11:16,942
I'm also inspired by
general-purpose service robots.

236
00:11:16,942 --> 00:11:18,400
If you come to my
lab, you'll often

237
00:11:18,400 --> 00:11:19,817
see robots like
this one just sort

238
00:11:19,817 --> 00:11:22,260
of wandering
through the hallways

239
00:11:22,260 --> 00:11:24,440
in this kind of a setting.

240
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:26,240
And actually, the
RoboCup competitions

241
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:29,140
involve general-purpose
service robots as well,

242
00:11:29,140 --> 00:11:33,652
doing things like
acting as a host, having

243
00:11:33,652 --> 00:11:39,160
to pick up items to set the
table, put away groceries.

244
00:11:39,160 --> 00:11:41,220
Here's the task
serving breakfast.

245
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:43,720
So there's a competition
called RoboCup@Home, trying

246
00:11:43,720 --> 00:11:48,460
to create robots that are
generally capable in this way,

247
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:51,040
and that's been very inspiring
to my research as well.

248
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:55,460
And then, like many, I've been
inspired by autonomous driving.

249
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,280
An autonomous car is an agent.

250
00:11:57,280 --> 00:11:58,880
It has to sense,
decide, and act.

251
00:11:58,880 --> 00:12:01,500
I had a car in the DARPA Urban
Challenge, the one pictured

252
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:02,940
here.

253
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:04,320
I don't have that car anymore.

254
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:07,740
As you know the car companies
and companies like Waymo

255
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:09,340
and Zoox are putting
a lot of money

256
00:12:09,340 --> 00:12:12,260
into making this a reality.

257
00:12:12,260 --> 00:12:13,980
But we've thought
over the course

258
00:12:13,980 --> 00:12:16,560
of several years, and
actually a couple decades now,

259
00:12:16,560 --> 00:12:19,060
what the world would look like
when all the cars on the road

260
00:12:19,060 --> 00:12:19,760
are autonomous?

261
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:22,040
Would we still need traffic
signals and stop signs,

262
00:12:22,040 --> 00:12:24,660
or will we be able to see
systems more like this?

263
00:12:24,660 --> 00:12:27,660
This was the basis for
Dresdner's thesis back in,

264
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:31,740
might have been 2010 or so.

265
00:12:31,740 --> 00:12:34,600
But this video, I think,
came out first in 2004.

266
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:36,920
And what's happening
is that each car

267
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:39,260
is an agent calling
ahead for a reservation

268
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:41,300
through the intersection.

269
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:43,580
And once it turns white,
it has a guaranteed path

270
00:12:43,580 --> 00:12:46,795
through the intersection
that won't interfere

271
00:12:46,795 --> 00:12:47,920
with any of the other cars.

272
00:12:47,920 --> 00:12:50,212
When they're yellow, they
don't have a reservation yet,

273
00:12:50,212 --> 00:12:52,040
so they often have to stop.

274
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:58,463
But this is when all
the cars are autonomous.

275
00:12:58,463 --> 00:13:00,380
We may be able to see
intersections like that.

276
00:13:00,380 --> 00:13:01,755
We've also looked
at what happens

277
00:13:01,755 --> 00:13:04,300
if you have human drivers
mixed with autonomous drivers

278
00:13:04,300 --> 00:13:06,440
and things like that.

279
00:13:06,440 --> 00:13:09,380
And so I could give a whole
talk on any of these topics.

280
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:11,320
Most recently, I
mentioned that I've

281
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:13,760
been inspired by these
challenges of trying

282
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:19,360
to get AI agents to be better
than humans at various tasks.

283
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:24,960
And so with my group at Sony AI,
we actually, a couple of years

284
00:13:24,960 --> 00:13:27,820
ago, contributed to
that stream of results.

285
00:13:27,820 --> 00:13:31,040
We had a paper on
the cover of Nature,

286
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:34,360
where we created
reinforcement learning agents

287
00:13:34,360 --> 00:13:38,000
that were able to drive faster
than the very best human drivers

288
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,680
at the game, Gran Turismo, which
is a very physically realistic

289
00:13:42,680 --> 00:13:47,000
simulation environment.

290
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:48,680
So this was the first
time-- the reason

291
00:13:48,680 --> 00:13:50,710
it was picked up
by Nature, this was

292
00:13:50,710 --> 00:13:52,990
the first time
that AI agents were

293
00:13:52,990 --> 00:13:56,030
better than humans at a
real-time control task, not

294
00:13:56,030 --> 00:14:01,150
turn-taking like poker and chess
and go and things like that,

295
00:14:01,150 --> 00:14:06,550
or I should say a physically
realistic real-time control

296
00:14:06,550 --> 00:14:08,150
task.

297
00:14:08,150 --> 00:14:10,590
And the one way to
get a sense of that

298
00:14:10,590 --> 00:14:15,910
is to watch above and below, an
expert human driver, Ken Chan,

299
00:14:15,910 --> 00:14:20,612
driving in the simulator in
the top half of the screen

300
00:14:20,612 --> 00:14:23,070
and in the real car on the
bottom half of the screen, where

301
00:14:23,070 --> 00:14:26,310
he's controlling the same car on
the same track in the simulator

302
00:14:26,310 --> 00:14:28,270
versus in the real world.

303
00:14:28,270 --> 00:14:32,030
And it models sufficiently
the slip stream and tire

304
00:14:32,030 --> 00:14:34,776
slip and traction
and things like that,

305
00:14:34,776 --> 00:14:38,150
so that he was able to finish
the driving at his best in both

306
00:14:38,150 --> 00:14:39,810
the real world
and the simulator.

307
00:14:39,810 --> 00:14:43,710
He finished the lap within a
10th of a second of the times

308
00:14:43,710 --> 00:14:46,190
in the two modalities.

309
00:14:46,190 --> 00:14:49,610
And there are people who have
trained in this simulator

310
00:14:49,610 --> 00:14:51,470
to become professional
race car drivers.

311
00:14:51,470 --> 00:14:57,690
And so we did a race against
four of the best human drivers.

312
00:14:57,690 --> 00:15:01,210
This was in October of 20-- we
first raced them in July of 2021

313
00:15:01,210 --> 00:15:02,310
and did not win--

314
00:15:02,310 --> 00:15:05,750
the humans were still
better than the AI agents.

315
00:15:05,750 --> 00:15:09,610
But then by October of
2021, there was an event

316
00:15:09,610 --> 00:15:13,010
that's the basis for the Nature
article from February of 2022,

317
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:17,130
where we beat the four of
the best drivers in the world

318
00:15:17,130 --> 00:15:21,290
who've put in their 10,000 hours
to become real experts on three

319
00:15:21,290 --> 00:15:24,687
different race car
track combinations.

320
00:15:24,687 --> 00:15:27,270
And since then, it's actually
been deployed in the video game.

321
00:15:27,270 --> 00:15:30,190
If you have a PlayStation
and the game Gran Turismo,

322
00:15:30,190 --> 00:15:32,430
you can race against GT Sophy.

323
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,690
There's been a lot of
research beyond the paper

324
00:15:34,690 --> 00:15:37,670
to make it more
engaging and fun.

325
00:15:37,670 --> 00:15:40,370
But fundamentally, this
was a success story

326
00:15:40,370 --> 00:15:43,410
for end-to-end reinforcement
learning, where

327
00:15:43,410 --> 00:15:46,370
the agent started acting
randomly, literally just driving

328
00:15:46,370 --> 00:15:49,750
in circles or backwards or off
the track, crashing into things

329
00:15:49,750 --> 00:15:53,950
and getting reward for
moving fast around the track.

330
00:15:53,950 --> 00:15:57,950
And after about
a day of training

331
00:15:57,950 --> 00:16:00,070
on a bank of
PlayStations, it was

332
00:16:00,070 --> 00:16:02,477
able to do better than the
best humans on an empty track,

333
00:16:02,477 --> 00:16:04,310
and after about a week,
better than the best

334
00:16:04,310 --> 00:16:07,830
humans in a competitive racing
scenario, where it learns skills

335
00:16:07,830 --> 00:16:14,130
like this one, where the colored
car is the agent GT Sophy,

336
00:16:14,130 --> 00:16:16,710
the white ones are
all human drivers.

337
00:16:16,710 --> 00:16:20,069
And here you see it learning to
take a defensive line, so it's

338
00:16:20,069 --> 00:16:22,050
not going the fastest
it can around the track,

339
00:16:22,050 --> 00:16:24,270
but rather positioning
itself in such a way

340
00:16:24,270 --> 00:16:26,089
that it's hard for
the humans to pass it.

341
00:16:26,089 --> 00:16:29,109
And it's learned to do
that entirely on its own.

342
00:16:29,109 --> 00:16:34,059
So this is work from a large
team of people at Sony.

343
00:16:34,059 --> 00:16:37,230

344
00:16:37,230 --> 00:16:41,430
And like I said, it's
ongoing, and it's something

345
00:16:41,430 --> 00:16:42,810
that we're really proud of.

346
00:16:42,810 --> 00:16:47,680
Most recently, with a different
team at Sony AI, in fact,

347
00:16:47,680 --> 00:16:49,520
very recently, this
is just last week,

348
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:51,937
or maybe within the last two
weeks that this was published

349
00:16:51,937 --> 00:16:59,420
in Nature, we contributed a new
human-centric image benchmark

350
00:16:59,420 --> 00:17:01,620
for--

351
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:04,000
basically, as I said
here in the tagline,

352
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,260
and this was led
by Alice Xiang It's

353
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:09,060
the first globally diverse,
consensually collected fairness

354
00:17:09,060 --> 00:17:11,540
evaluation data set
for a wide variety

355
00:17:11,540 --> 00:17:13,800
of human-centric
computer vision tasks.

356
00:17:13,800 --> 00:17:16,540
So really what sets
it apart, our goals

357
00:17:16,540 --> 00:17:19,580
were to identify the
ethical and legal issues

358
00:17:19,580 --> 00:17:22,740
of creating data sets,
rather than just scraping

359
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:27,700
YouTube and Instagram and Reddit
and things that go into training

360
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:30,040
these large models.

361
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:34,660
Our goal was to try to, at least
at the scale that you could

362
00:17:34,660 --> 00:17:39,740
for evaluation, create
a data set where

363
00:17:39,740 --> 00:17:41,860
all of the participants,
everybody who appears

364
00:17:41,860 --> 00:17:44,040
in the pictures,
has been compensated

365
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:47,320
and has self-identified
what the label should

366
00:17:47,320 --> 00:17:50,120
be that describe them.

367
00:17:50,120 --> 00:17:53,520
And so tried to
start with FHIBE,

368
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:56,080
tried to establish an industry
standard for AI ethics.

369
00:17:56,080 --> 00:18:00,760
And also, it's built
ethically, but also

370
00:18:00,760 --> 00:18:05,360
to evaluate bias in AI models.

371
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:10,438
And so in a way of
creating, and I'm not

372
00:18:10,438 --> 00:18:11,980
going to read through
the slide here,

373
00:18:11,980 --> 00:18:15,760
but you can see some of
the features of FHIBE.

374
00:18:15,760 --> 00:18:17,860
And it comprises 10,000 images.

375
00:18:17,860 --> 00:18:20,060
So it's not enough
to train a model on,

376
00:18:20,060 --> 00:18:24,660
but it's enough to evaluate
a model for biases.

377
00:18:24,660 --> 00:18:28,960
It's the first one that
goes across the whole global

378
00:18:28,960 --> 00:18:32,080
geography, 81
countries and areas,

379
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:34,500
and categorizes a whole
bunch of different features,

380
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:36,680
as you can see here.

381
00:18:36,680 --> 00:18:40,020
And annotations of the
camera that are used,

382
00:18:40,020 --> 00:18:43,500
the environment,
and the features

383
00:18:43,500 --> 00:18:46,700
of the people in
the image itself,

384
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:50,072
as labeled by the
person who appears

385
00:18:50,072 --> 00:18:53,420
in the image in many cases.

386
00:18:53,420 --> 00:18:55,860
And so we use this in
the Nature article, which

387
00:18:55,860 --> 00:18:59,500
you can find online
now do analysis

388
00:18:59,500 --> 00:19:03,700
of the sorts of biases that
happen in visual language

389
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:04,200
models.

390
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:06,580
And I verified some existing
ones that were known

391
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:11,300
and also identified
some new ones.

392
00:19:11,300 --> 00:19:16,940
So that's, in a nutshell, the
kinds of things that inspire me

393
00:19:16,940 --> 00:19:21,600
and the research that I do have.

394
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:23,340
As I said, you have--

395
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:26,200
another theme that I think
comes up a lot in agents,

396
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:29,180
and you've heard a little bit
about during this semester,

397
00:19:29,180 --> 00:19:34,140
is human-in-the-loop learning,
which is learning from

398
00:19:34,140 --> 00:19:36,660
interaction.

399
00:19:36,660 --> 00:19:39,940
And there's a system
that was developed

400
00:19:39,940 --> 00:19:42,970
by a former student of mine,
Brad Knox, who's now faculty

401
00:19:42,970 --> 00:19:50,890
here at UT Austin, that was a
system called TAMER, Teaching

402
00:19:50,890 --> 00:19:54,970
an Agent Manually via
Evaluative Reinforcement.

403
00:19:54,970 --> 00:19:59,170
And the idea here, it was
the-- actually, I think,

404
00:19:59,170 --> 00:20:01,570
it has a claim to
being the first system

405
00:20:01,570 --> 00:20:03,870
to do reinforcement learning
from human feedback,

406
00:20:03,870 --> 00:20:08,210
or RLHF, which is a key point, a
key aspect of training language

407
00:20:08,210 --> 00:20:09,210
models now.

408
00:20:09,210 --> 00:20:13,890
But his core demonstration
was in the game of Tetris

409
00:20:13,890 --> 00:20:16,380
that you see here.

410
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:18,130
Most of you are probably
familiar-- you're

411
00:20:18,130 --> 00:20:19,917
trying to clear rows.

412
00:20:19,917 --> 00:20:21,750
And what's happening
in the top-left corner,

413
00:20:21,750 --> 00:20:24,510
a person is saying, good move
or bad move-- green for good,

414
00:20:24,510 --> 00:20:26,210
and red for bad.

415
00:20:26,210 --> 00:20:29,250
And at first, it was random.

416
00:20:29,250 --> 00:20:32,270
This was a task that at
the time of the system,

417
00:20:32,270 --> 00:20:34,210
you could do with
reinforcement learning

418
00:20:34,210 --> 00:20:37,650
if you trained for hundreds
of thousands of games.

419
00:20:37,650 --> 00:20:41,090
But Brad was able to show
that even in the first game,

420
00:20:41,090 --> 00:20:42,750
with this human
feedback, you can start

421
00:20:42,750 --> 00:20:44,050
doing much better than random.

422
00:20:44,050 --> 00:20:48,310
So it's already starting
to clear some lines.

423
00:20:48,310 --> 00:20:49,750
Oops.

424
00:20:49,750 --> 00:20:55,030
And then by the third
game, it's able to--

425
00:20:55,030 --> 00:20:57,977
so this is after just
two games of a person--

426
00:20:57,977 --> 00:21:00,310
two episodes of a person
providing positive and negative

427
00:21:00,310 --> 00:21:00,850
feedback.

428
00:21:00,850 --> 00:21:03,250
It's already able to
clear hundreds of lines.

429
00:21:03,250 --> 00:21:06,470
And so this was one of the
early examples or demonstrations

430
00:21:06,470 --> 00:21:10,630
of the power of learning
from human interaction,

431
00:21:10,630 --> 00:21:12,490
as opposed to just
random exploration.

432
00:21:12,490 --> 00:21:15,610
GT Sophy was purely
from random exploration.

433
00:21:15,610 --> 00:21:17,170
It was all
reinforcement learning.

434
00:21:17,170 --> 00:21:22,230
This is reinforcement
learning from human feedback.

435
00:21:22,230 --> 00:21:23,990
And one of the key
lessons here was

436
00:21:23,990 --> 00:21:27,790
that TAMER learns more
quickly than autonomous

437
00:21:27,790 --> 00:21:30,710
learning from straight
reinforcement learning.

438
00:21:30,710 --> 00:21:32,690
It doesn't take hundreds
of thousands of games.

439
00:21:32,690 --> 00:21:35,730
It takes three games to
get to quite a good level,

440
00:21:35,730 --> 00:21:37,830
but ultimately
doesn't do as well

441
00:21:37,830 --> 00:21:40,370
as if you allow
for reinforcement

442
00:21:40,370 --> 00:21:42,790
learning to learn from scratch.

443
00:21:42,790 --> 00:21:45,730
You may have heard of the
bitter lesson from Rich Sutton

444
00:21:45,730 --> 00:21:50,130
that injecting human
input could bring down

445
00:21:50,130 --> 00:21:51,600
the ultimate performance.

446
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:53,350
But there are also
ways of combining them.

447
00:21:53,350 --> 00:21:56,790
So we had an award-winning
paper TAMER plus RL,

448
00:21:56,790 --> 00:21:59,970
which learned both from human
feedback and autonomously.

449
00:21:59,970 --> 00:22:03,410
And this was also with Brad
Knox, part of his thesis.

450
00:22:03,410 --> 00:22:06,450
Then collaborating with Garrett
Warnell, pictured on the right,

451
00:22:06,450 --> 00:22:10,490
we had an extension to
video or image input,

452
00:22:10,490 --> 00:22:11,990
a system called Deep Tamer.

453
00:22:11,990 --> 00:22:15,570
And like I said, this
really was, I think,

454
00:22:15,570 --> 00:22:20,970
the first example of RLHF,
which has really been

455
00:22:20,970 --> 00:22:23,020
super impactful lately.

456
00:22:23,020 --> 00:22:25,850

457
00:22:25,850 --> 00:22:31,750
And then this has been
extended to also learning,

458
00:22:31,750 --> 00:22:33,610
not just from explicit
feedback but learning

459
00:22:33,610 --> 00:22:34,910
from implicit feedback.

460
00:22:34,910 --> 00:22:37,890
So not just a person explicitly
saying good move or bad move,

461
00:22:37,890 --> 00:22:40,270
but you can imagine if
you were an autonomous car

462
00:22:40,270 --> 00:22:41,890
and your passengers
looked like this,

463
00:22:41,890 --> 00:22:43,170
you wouldn't need
somebody to tell

464
00:22:43,170 --> 00:22:45,430
you-- shouldn't need somebody
to tell you that you've

465
00:22:45,430 --> 00:22:46,410
done something wrong.

466
00:22:46,410 --> 00:22:47,750
You've scared them.

467
00:22:47,750 --> 00:22:51,530
And so this should allow you to
learn from implicit feedback.

468
00:22:51,530 --> 00:22:54,630
So we, a couple years
ago, in a coral paper,

469
00:22:54,630 --> 00:22:58,750
introduced a system called
EMPATHIC, for Evaluative MaPping

470
00:22:58,750 --> 00:23:02,170
for Effective Task-learning
via Human Implicit Cues,

471
00:23:02,170 --> 00:23:04,480
but it's about learning from
implicit human feedback.

472
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:06,990

473
00:23:06,990 --> 00:23:12,870
And then we've gone on to think
about all the different ways in,

474
00:23:12,870 --> 00:23:17,790
or many of the different ways,
in which humans can provide

475
00:23:17,790 --> 00:23:25,750
input in machine learning
through the robot navigation

476
00:23:25,750 --> 00:23:29,230
problem, where a robot
has to start at a point

477
00:23:29,230 --> 00:23:33,085
and get to a global goal
while avoiding obstacles.

478
00:23:33,085 --> 00:23:35,210
I'm not going to go into
the details on this again,

479
00:23:35,210 --> 00:23:40,540
I'm just going to show
you the punch line where

480
00:23:40,540 --> 00:23:43,020
we were-- but it's trying
to learn to have the robots

481
00:23:43,020 --> 00:23:46,980
move through very
constrained spaces, which

482
00:23:46,980 --> 00:23:48,500
is not a solved problem yet.

483
00:23:48,500 --> 00:23:52,500
So it's navigation, is pretty
well known how to do it.

484
00:23:52,500 --> 00:23:56,380
But if you're trying to navigate
through very constrained spaces

485
00:23:56,380 --> 00:23:59,700
that are not much
bigger than the robot,

486
00:23:59,700 --> 00:24:02,600
there are navigation
packages out there--

487
00:24:02,600 --> 00:24:07,280
oops, I wanted to go to
the previous slide here.

488
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:09,160
Yeah, there's navigation
packages out there,

489
00:24:09,160 --> 00:24:12,580
but they require lots of opaque
parameters, things like this,

490
00:24:12,580 --> 00:24:14,940
that you wouldn't
really know how to tune,

491
00:24:14,940 --> 00:24:16,580
if you wanted the
robot to work well.

492
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:18,622
And they're different in
very different settings,

493
00:24:18,622 --> 00:24:20,840
whether the robot's in a
corridor or an open space

494
00:24:20,840 --> 00:24:24,060
or an obstacle
course or a pinch.

495
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:28,380
And so right now, if
you want to get a robot

496
00:24:28,380 --> 00:24:31,240
to navigate effectively
in these kinds of spaces,

497
00:24:31,240 --> 00:24:35,320
it takes a roboticist to
fine-tune those planning

498
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:36,860
parameters, or at least it did.

499
00:24:36,860 --> 00:24:43,940
And so we explored various
ways of using human input

500
00:24:43,940 --> 00:24:47,060
to allow for a robot to
learn how to do this.

501
00:24:47,060 --> 00:24:50,080
So ranging from a teleoperated
demonstration where a person is

502
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:52,520
literally just
remote-controlling the robot,

503
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:55,502
and then we have the robot
explore in parameter space

504
00:24:55,502 --> 00:24:56,960
to come up with
the parameters that

505
00:24:56,960 --> 00:25:01,840
would lead to the most similar
behavior to the slightly less

506
00:25:01,840 --> 00:25:04,760
demanding corrective information
or interventions where

507
00:25:04,760 --> 00:25:08,500
a person doesn't need to always
be remote-controlling the robot,

508
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:11,870
but can just step in when the
robot is doing something wrong.

509
00:25:11,870 --> 00:25:14,120
And again, now the robot can
try to emulate or predict

510
00:25:14,120 --> 00:25:17,360
when a person would
step in to intervene.

511
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:21,400
We had a system for doing it
from evaluative feedback, which

512
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:25,960
is much closer to the TAMER
system, where the person just

513
00:25:25,960 --> 00:25:27,460
has to say good
move or bad move,

514
00:25:27,460 --> 00:25:29,040
give positive and
negative feedback,

515
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:31,940
all the way up to
reinforcement learning systems.

516
00:25:31,940 --> 00:25:34,660
And so you can also
find a full talk

517
00:25:34,660 --> 00:25:38,020
on my website about the various
different technical algorithms

518
00:25:38,020 --> 00:25:40,580
behind doing this
Adaptive Planner Parameter

519
00:25:40,580 --> 00:25:44,100
Learning, where
rather than learning

520
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:48,600
all of the different parameters
of a navigation stack,

521
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:50,860
we're instead treating
that navigation stack

522
00:25:50,860 --> 00:25:56,460
like a black box and learning
just the parameters that

523
00:25:56,460 --> 00:25:59,540
will lead to the
behavior that you want.

524
00:25:59,540 --> 00:26:03,940
And so the way I think of
this is in the language

525
00:26:03,940 --> 00:26:05,340
of machine learning.

526
00:26:05,340 --> 00:26:06,920
We're learning a
parameter policy.

527
00:26:06,920 --> 00:26:08,960
Rather than outputting
the raw actions,

528
00:26:08,960 --> 00:26:11,540
we're outputting the
parameters to a navigation

529
00:26:11,540 --> 00:26:13,980
stack or a classical
motion planner,

530
00:26:13,980 --> 00:26:18,220
and trying to learn which
ones lead to the actions

531
00:26:18,220 --> 00:26:19,310
that you most desire.

532
00:26:19,310 --> 00:26:25,126

533
00:26:25,126 --> 00:26:27,580
I think that's where
I'll stop in terms

534
00:26:27,580 --> 00:26:30,870
of telling you almost nothing
about a lot of different things.

535
00:26:30,870 --> 00:26:34,050

536
00:26:34,050 --> 00:26:36,810
Like I said, when I
looked through the topics

537
00:26:36,810 --> 00:26:40,870
that you've covered so
far, there have been,

538
00:26:40,870 --> 00:26:43,910
I think, less on the
physical robots than

539
00:26:43,910 --> 00:26:46,330
on the multi-agent systems
and the reinforcement

540
00:26:46,330 --> 00:26:50,090
learning that I
focused on a lot.

541
00:26:50,090 --> 00:26:51,990
Oh, actually, sorry,
I take that back.

542
00:26:51,990 --> 00:26:54,157
There was one more thing I
wanted to tell you almost

543
00:26:54,157 --> 00:26:58,450
nothing about, which is,
on the interaction side,

544
00:26:58,450 --> 00:27:02,850
a problem that's fascinated
me over the past,

545
00:27:02,850 --> 00:27:06,890
decade-and-a-half or so,
called ad hoc teamwork,

546
00:27:06,890 --> 00:27:09,310
which is a particular
type of interaction,

547
00:27:09,310 --> 00:27:14,970
where the goal is to try to
create an individual agent that

548
00:27:14,970 --> 00:27:17,730
is good at cooperating
with teammates,

549
00:27:17,730 --> 00:27:20,730
but you don't necessarily
get to, or you don't at all,

550
00:27:20,730 --> 00:27:22,470
get to control who
those teammates are.

551
00:27:22,470 --> 00:27:24,730
The kind of thing that
people can really do well

552
00:27:24,730 --> 00:27:28,150
when they're playing pick-up
soccer or pick-up basketball,

553
00:27:28,150 --> 00:27:34,710
but now we want robots to
be able to do if there were

554
00:27:34,710 --> 00:27:37,310
a disaster-rescue scenario
and lots of different people

555
00:27:37,310 --> 00:27:39,190
brought the robots
together, we don't

556
00:27:39,190 --> 00:27:41,410
want to have to reprogram
them to work as a team.

557
00:27:41,410 --> 00:27:44,550
We want them to be able to
figure out on-the-fly how

558
00:27:44,550 --> 00:27:46,010
to be able to work together.

559
00:27:46,010 --> 00:27:47,910
And so there's a bunch
of work in my lab

560
00:27:47,910 --> 00:27:53,030
also on this problem of creating
a good team player, which

561
00:27:53,030 --> 00:27:56,310
is, I think, a part of the
multi-agent systems challenge

562
00:27:56,310 --> 00:27:58,310
or the interaction challenge.

563
00:27:58,310 --> 00:28:03,030
And there's a challenge
paper from AAAI 2010

564
00:28:03,030 --> 00:28:05,450
that introduces ad hoc
teamwork as a problem.

565
00:28:05,450 --> 00:28:08,950
And then there's been a
bunch of theoretical research

566
00:28:08,950 --> 00:28:13,030
and experimental
research on the topic.

567
00:28:13,030 --> 00:28:15,850
OK, now, I really do--

568
00:28:15,850 --> 00:28:20,070
that's the whirlwind
part of this talk, where

569
00:28:20,070 --> 00:28:23,875
I've told you very little about
a lot of work that pertains

570
00:28:23,875 --> 00:28:25,750
to machine learning,
especially reinforcement

571
00:28:25,750 --> 00:28:28,770
learning, and
interaction, especially

572
00:28:28,770 --> 00:28:30,830
ad hoc teamwork,
multi-agent systems.

573
00:28:30,830 --> 00:28:34,970
But you've seen some coverage of
those in the semester already.

574
00:28:34,970 --> 00:28:37,330
With regards to
embodiment, I'm going

575
00:28:37,330 --> 00:28:39,850
to take a little
bit of a deeper dive

576
00:28:39,850 --> 00:28:44,170
and show you a paper from just--
it was presented at CoRL just

577
00:28:44,170 --> 00:28:46,530
about a month ago.

578
00:28:46,530 --> 00:28:49,020
And let me put this
into full-screen mode.

579
00:28:49,020 --> 00:28:52,140

580
00:28:52,140 --> 00:28:53,140
Why is this not working?

581
00:28:53,140 --> 00:28:57,290

582
00:28:57,290 --> 00:29:00,170
Try it again.

583
00:29:00,170 --> 00:29:03,080
And this is the goal of--

584
00:29:03,080 --> 00:29:08,130

585
00:29:08,130 --> 00:29:14,010
I'm sorry, this
should be coming up.

586
00:29:14,010 --> 00:29:18,610
It's the goal of trying to
do reinforcement learning--

587
00:29:18,610 --> 00:29:19,350
so there we go--

588
00:29:19,350 --> 00:29:24,610

589
00:29:24,610 --> 00:29:27,751
in the real world.

590
00:29:27,751 --> 00:29:33,080
So the motivation here is, and
I should say this is the PhD

591
00:29:33,080 --> 00:29:37,960
thesis work of Jiaheng
Hu, shown here,

592
00:29:37,960 --> 00:29:41,140
and he's co-advised by myself
and Roberto Martín-Martin.

593
00:29:41,140 --> 00:29:42,640
And this paper was
published, like I

594
00:29:42,640 --> 00:29:46,280
said at CoRL, the Conference
on Robot Learning recently.

595
00:29:46,280 --> 00:29:53,440
And so the paper is SLAC, for
Simulation-Pretrained Latent

596
00:29:53,440 --> 00:29:56,400
Action Space for Whole
Body Real-World RL.

597
00:29:56,400 --> 00:30:02,880
And so the key here is
trying to do real-world RL.

598
00:30:02,880 --> 00:30:06,725
I want to get rid of
that little thing there.

599
00:30:06,725 --> 00:30:08,100
No, that's not
what I want to do.

600
00:30:08,100 --> 00:30:08,600
I'm sorry.

601
00:30:08,600 --> 00:30:11,000
This is not going
exactly how I was hoping.

602
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,700
Let's just keep it in this mode.

603
00:30:13,700 --> 00:30:17,920
So it's learning visuo-motor,
contact-rich, whole-body mobile

604
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:22,960
manipulation with real-world
reinforcement learning

605
00:30:22,960 --> 00:30:24,540
without a single demonstration.

606
00:30:24,540 --> 00:30:28,500
So when you're doing
learning in robotics,

607
00:30:28,500 --> 00:30:31,340
you can't do what we
did with GT Sophy, which

608
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:35,192
was learn on a bank
of PlayStations,

609
00:30:35,192 --> 00:30:38,300
hundreds of thousands or
millions of interactions.

610
00:30:38,300 --> 00:30:41,940
While we were all sleeping,
the computers would be working.

611
00:30:41,940 --> 00:30:43,520
When you're working
on a real robot,

612
00:30:43,520 --> 00:30:46,526
you have to work
very, very efficiently

613
00:30:46,526 --> 00:30:48,820
with very little data.

614
00:30:48,820 --> 00:30:54,500
And so you have to
learn very efficiently.

615
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:57,540
And a motivating
task here is we want

616
00:30:57,540 --> 00:30:59,480
to do whole-body
mobile manipulation.

617
00:30:59,480 --> 00:31:05,020
So this is a task such
as wiping the blue off

618
00:31:05,020 --> 00:31:09,140
of the whiteboard while avoiding
the obstacle of this trash band.

619
00:31:09,140 --> 00:31:10,780
And so it requires
the robot to know

620
00:31:10,780 --> 00:31:17,312
how to move to avoid
obstacles and not just to--

621
00:31:17,312 --> 00:31:21,140
the standard way to do this
with reinforcement learning

622
00:31:21,140 --> 00:31:25,680
would be to just explore lots
and lots of random actions

623
00:31:25,680 --> 00:31:26,180
first.

624
00:31:26,180 --> 00:31:29,680
And you can do that if
you have a simulator.

625
00:31:29,680 --> 00:31:32,740
But it's costly to build
high-fidelity simulators.

626
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:36,160
It's sometimes infeasible to
build a simulator that simulates

627
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:40,720
your robot well enough that if
you just learn in simulation

628
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:43,500
and apply the policy in the
real world, it would just work.

629
00:31:43,500 --> 00:31:45,760
There have been some
successes of that,

630
00:31:45,760 --> 00:31:49,040
it's not always straightforward.

631
00:31:49,040 --> 00:31:51,720
And so, we want to try to
learn without the benefit

632
00:31:51,720 --> 00:31:54,415
of the learning happening
in the simulator.

633
00:31:54,415 --> 00:31:56,040
One way people often
get around that is

634
00:31:56,040 --> 00:31:57,920
through imitation learning
or some kind of learning

635
00:31:57,920 --> 00:32:00,337
from human feedback, as I
showed with the adaptive planner

636
00:32:00,337 --> 00:32:01,920
parameter learning.

637
00:32:01,920 --> 00:32:04,520
But that can be very
costly to collect

638
00:32:04,520 --> 00:32:06,980
and the high quality
demonstrations.

639
00:32:06,980 --> 00:32:10,280
So our goal is to do real-world
reinforcement learning

640
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:13,720
with directly learning
from real interactions

641
00:32:13,720 --> 00:32:17,480
without this high-fidelity
simulation or demonstrations.

642
00:32:17,480 --> 00:32:22,400
And so, like I said, if you just
use a standard state-of-the-art

643
00:32:22,400 --> 00:32:26,910
reinforcement learning method,
you're going to just flail.

644
00:32:26,910 --> 00:32:29,310
So if you're trying to
learn to wipe the blue mark

645
00:32:29,310 --> 00:32:31,890
and you're getting reward
for wiping the blue mark,

646
00:32:31,890 --> 00:32:33,630
and you're just
sending random commands

647
00:32:33,630 --> 00:32:36,930
to all the motors of the
robot moving the base around,

648
00:32:36,930 --> 00:32:39,075
randomly moving the
arm around randomly,

649
00:32:39,075 --> 00:32:40,450
you're not going
to get very far.

650
00:32:40,450 --> 00:32:43,228
It's going to probably the
battery's going to die,

651
00:32:43,228 --> 00:32:45,270
or you're going to lose
patience before it learns

652
00:32:45,270 --> 00:32:47,270
to do anything correctly.

653
00:32:47,270 --> 00:32:49,950
And so there's a huge
challenge for trying

654
00:32:49,950 --> 00:32:54,790
to learn with sufficient
sample efficiency and safety

655
00:32:54,790 --> 00:32:56,630
in the real world.

656
00:32:56,630 --> 00:33:01,170
And so the system that Jiaheng
came up with is, like I said,

657
00:33:01,170 --> 00:33:02,190
it's called SLAC.

658
00:33:02,190 --> 00:33:06,750
And the key here is that
we need a good action

659
00:33:06,750 --> 00:33:11,150
space to facilitate
sample efficiency

660
00:33:11,150 --> 00:33:12,610
and safety in the real world.

661
00:33:12,610 --> 00:33:16,450
If we have, rather than sending
raw commands to the robot,

662
00:33:16,450 --> 00:33:19,650
but have a much more
abstract action space,

663
00:33:19,650 --> 00:33:22,090
then there's much
less to explore,

664
00:33:22,090 --> 00:33:25,090
and we're going to use
a low-fidelity simulator

665
00:33:25,090 --> 00:33:27,770
to come up with that
latent action space.

666
00:33:27,770 --> 00:33:31,210
So we're going to assume that
it's not that hard to come up

667
00:33:31,210 --> 00:33:36,550
with a rough simulator
of the robot,

668
00:33:36,550 --> 00:33:40,050
and that we can
then use that to--

669
00:33:40,050 --> 00:33:43,409
so here's a picture of what a
low-fidelity simulator might

670
00:33:43,409 --> 00:33:43,909
look like.

671
00:33:43,909 --> 00:33:45,270
So it's sort of
models the robot,

672
00:33:45,270 --> 00:33:46,645
but it's not going
to be perfect.

673
00:33:46,645 --> 00:33:49,156

674
00:33:49,156 --> 00:33:53,610
And we want to use what's known
as unsupervised reinforcement

675
00:33:53,610 --> 00:33:56,570
learning to learn
task-agnostic behaviors

676
00:33:56,570 --> 00:33:57,870
in a low-fidelity simulator.

677
00:33:57,870 --> 00:34:00,010
So what do I mean by
unsupervised reinforcement

678
00:34:00,010 --> 00:34:00,630
learning?

679
00:34:00,630 --> 00:34:05,090
That means giving
a robot or an agent

680
00:34:05,090 --> 00:34:09,790
an environment to explore in,
but no reward signal, no task.

681
00:34:09,790 --> 00:34:12,130
So it's unsupervised because
there's no reward signal,

682
00:34:12,130 --> 00:34:16,170
and get it to try to just
figure out all of the actions

683
00:34:16,170 --> 00:34:19,130
that it might want to
take, that might be useful

684
00:34:19,130 --> 00:34:22,630
when it is given a reward
function downstream

685
00:34:22,630 --> 00:34:23,370
in the future.

686
00:34:23,370 --> 00:34:28,310
So the kind of thing where
we put a baby in a playpen--

687
00:34:28,310 --> 00:34:30,190
a human baby in a
playpen, they tend

688
00:34:30,190 --> 00:34:34,990
to try stacking blocks
up or squeezing things

689
00:34:34,990 --> 00:34:36,390
to see what noise they make.

690
00:34:36,390 --> 00:34:39,470
They don't really
have any task to do,

691
00:34:39,470 --> 00:34:41,470
but they're, in
some sense, training

692
00:34:41,470 --> 00:34:43,910
themselves to do things
that might be useful

693
00:34:43,910 --> 00:34:45,455
once they are given tasks.

694
00:34:45,455 --> 00:34:46,830
And so that's the
idea, is trying

695
00:34:46,830 --> 00:34:49,810
to get a robot to do
the same sort of thing.

696
00:34:49,810 --> 00:34:52,710
That's what the unsupervised
reinforcement learning idea is.

697
00:34:52,710 --> 00:34:56,030
And the goal here is to come
up with the types of actions

698
00:34:56,030 --> 00:34:59,230
that you might
want a robot to do.

699
00:34:59,230 --> 00:35:02,350
And so what we're trying
to learn is a latent action

700
00:35:02,350 --> 00:35:05,850
space where you give just--

701
00:35:05,850 --> 00:35:08,070
you define a number
of dimensions,

702
00:35:08,070 --> 00:35:13,430
some small set of
dimensions, and then

703
00:35:13,430 --> 00:35:20,680
try to make it so that when
you change the latent actions,

704
00:35:20,680 --> 00:35:27,050
you get meaningfully different
behaviors in the real world

705
00:35:27,050 --> 00:35:33,380
so that this latent
action space can create--

706
00:35:33,380 --> 00:35:35,620
you might imagine
that the robot would

707
00:35:35,620 --> 00:35:39,140
learn to get close to the
whiteboard versus getting

708
00:35:39,140 --> 00:35:40,440
far away from the whiteboard.

709
00:35:40,440 --> 00:35:41,900
Those are something
that you can do

710
00:35:41,900 --> 00:35:44,420
by just moving the
base of the robot,

711
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:46,500
or you could imagine
another action

712
00:35:46,500 --> 00:35:51,180
dimension controlling the arm
or where the camera is pointing.

713
00:35:51,180 --> 00:35:53,620
And so we came up with
a method called DUSD,

714
00:35:53,620 --> 00:35:56,820
or disentangled unsupervised
skill discovery,

715
00:35:56,820 --> 00:35:59,460
and this was before
the SLAC paper, that

716
00:35:59,460 --> 00:36:03,900
does learn this sort of diverse,
temporally extended behaviors

717
00:36:03,900 --> 00:36:05,840
by trying to come up with--

718
00:36:05,840 --> 00:36:09,900
by maximizing or minimizing
the mutual information

719
00:36:09,900 --> 00:36:15,432
between the behaviors
you get when you change

720
00:36:15,432 --> 00:36:17,640
the latent action features.

721
00:36:17,640 --> 00:36:19,735
There's been previous work.

722
00:36:19,735 --> 00:36:21,860
There was a paper called
Diversity is All You Need,

723
00:36:21,860 --> 00:36:24,080
or DIAYN, from Sergey
Levine's group that

724
00:36:24,080 --> 00:36:26,065
had that insight initially.

725
00:36:26,065 --> 00:36:27,440
But one of the
things we added is

726
00:36:27,440 --> 00:36:30,800
that we tried to do it
in a disentangled way.

727
00:36:30,800 --> 00:36:34,880
So to create a
structured action space,

728
00:36:34,880 --> 00:36:40,480
by which we mean that if you
vary one latent variable,

729
00:36:40,480 --> 00:36:43,800
it only changes one
dimension of the action space

730
00:36:43,800 --> 00:36:45,220
and also adds safety.

731
00:36:45,220 --> 00:36:51,040
So what I mean by that is we
want to add two different reward

732
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:51,980
signals.

733
00:36:51,980 --> 00:36:56,240
One is such that if you change
one of the latent action

734
00:36:56,240 --> 00:37:00,780
features, it only changes one
aspect of the robot's behavior.

735
00:37:00,780 --> 00:37:03,680
So here the red indicates
where the camera is pointing

736
00:37:03,680 --> 00:37:06,920
and the blue indicates
where the arm is moving

737
00:37:06,920 --> 00:37:09,220
and the red indicates
where the bass is moving.

738
00:37:09,220 --> 00:37:11,800
We wanted sort of a nice
structured action space

739
00:37:11,800 --> 00:37:13,840
so that you can--

740
00:37:13,840 --> 00:37:15,480
if you vary one of
the latent action

741
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:17,600
features and hold
the others constant,

742
00:37:17,600 --> 00:37:21,245
it'll only move the base,
the arm of the camera.

743
00:37:21,245 --> 00:37:22,620
And so that was
something that we

744
00:37:22,620 --> 00:37:26,340
were able to do using DUSD,
using what we call the skill

745
00:37:26,340 --> 00:37:28,500
empowerment reward.

746
00:37:28,500 --> 00:37:32,260
And then we also added a
hand-coded safety reward

747
00:37:32,260 --> 00:37:35,940
that's like don't collide with
yourself, something like that.

748
00:37:35,940 --> 00:37:39,700
And when you put those together,
you can come up with a--

749
00:37:39,700 --> 00:37:44,020
train the action decoder in
this and the latent actions

750
00:37:44,020 --> 00:37:46,740
such that you get this
structured and safe set

751
00:37:46,740 --> 00:37:50,300
of actions in the
low-fidelity simulator.

752
00:37:50,300 --> 00:37:55,460
And then once you have that
in the second step of SLAC,

753
00:37:55,460 --> 00:37:58,500
you can then take the
learned latent action space

754
00:37:58,500 --> 00:38:03,060
and use it for downstream task
learning in the real world.

755
00:38:03,060 --> 00:38:06,220
And so, again, you have this
lower dimensional action space

756
00:38:06,220 --> 00:38:09,960
where each dimension changes the
different aspect of the robot.

757
00:38:09,960 --> 00:38:12,660
And so then we're going
to separately develop

758
00:38:12,660 --> 00:38:15,150
a factorized version
of Soft Actor-Critic.

759
00:38:15,150 --> 00:38:17,850
Soft Actor-Critic is one of the
popular reinforcement learning

760
00:38:17,850 --> 00:38:18,430
algorithms.

761
00:38:18,430 --> 00:38:21,170
It's actually the basis for the
one that we used in GT Sophy

762
00:38:21,170 --> 00:38:22,850
as well.

763
00:38:22,850 --> 00:38:28,250
And it leverages the structure
of the latent action space.

764
00:38:28,250 --> 00:38:29,970
And so what I mean
by that is that once

765
00:38:29,970 --> 00:38:35,850
you're given a reward, once you
know what your reward terms are,

766
00:38:35,850 --> 00:38:41,210
you might have a reward for
where the robot is located

767
00:38:41,210 --> 00:38:43,707
that can be controlled
by where the base is,

768
00:38:43,707 --> 00:38:46,290
a reward for where the robot is
looking that can be controlled

769
00:38:46,290 --> 00:38:49,530
by where the camera is pointing,
a reward for what you're

770
00:38:49,530 --> 00:38:54,410
picking up or touching, which is
controlled by where the arm is.

771
00:38:54,410 --> 00:38:58,410
The idea is that not
every latent factor

772
00:38:58,410 --> 00:39:00,270
controls every reward term.

773
00:39:00,270 --> 00:39:03,810
So you can get a much
sparser and more accurate

774
00:39:03,810 --> 00:39:07,610
prediction of the value function
in reinforcement learning terms,

775
00:39:07,610 --> 00:39:13,910
if you can learn to separate
which action factors control

776
00:39:13,910 --> 00:39:16,350
which reward terms.

777
00:39:16,350 --> 00:39:22,390
And so we did this in a paper
that appeared at RSS 2023.

778
00:39:22,390 --> 00:39:30,374
We used a conditional
mutual information metric

779
00:39:30,374 --> 00:39:36,150
that allows us to say, well,
if I change a particular action

780
00:39:36,150 --> 00:39:43,230
dimension, how does it change
the reward factors that I get.

781
00:39:43,230 --> 00:39:45,350
And so you can learn
a masking matrix

782
00:39:45,350 --> 00:39:48,470
that says, if you want to-- if
you have a reward term that only

783
00:39:48,470 --> 00:39:52,430
depends on where the base of the
robot is, which action factors

784
00:39:52,430 --> 00:39:56,730
do you need to vary, or can
you vary to get that reward,

785
00:39:56,730 --> 00:39:59,570
and so on for
every other factor.

786
00:39:59,570 --> 00:40:02,587
I know I'm going quickly here
from a technical perspective.

787
00:40:02,587 --> 00:40:04,170
I'm not going through
all of the math,

788
00:40:04,170 --> 00:40:06,030
but you can find this
in the papers that

789
00:40:06,030 --> 00:40:09,336
are referenced on the slide.

790
00:40:09,336 --> 00:40:13,130
And so then we can
use all the data

791
00:40:13,130 --> 00:40:16,570
that's used to infer which
action factors affect which

792
00:40:16,570 --> 00:40:20,710
reward terms for learning.

793
00:40:20,710 --> 00:40:23,890
And I'll get to the punchline
and the video demonstrations

794
00:40:23,890 --> 00:40:25,090
here coming up.

795
00:40:25,090 --> 00:40:28,130
We can take some
real-world tasks

796
00:40:28,130 --> 00:40:34,290
and then do learning entirely
from the onboard observations

797
00:40:34,290 --> 00:40:36,770
from the robot's camera
and proprioception, which

798
00:40:36,770 --> 00:40:40,050
is where the joint angles
are at any given time.

799
00:40:40,050 --> 00:40:42,250
And now learn a
task policy, the one

800
00:40:42,250 --> 00:40:45,070
that actually gets rewards for
the task, like in this case,

801
00:40:45,070 --> 00:40:48,850
it's sweeping
something into a basket

802
00:40:48,850 --> 00:40:51,970
with your frozen pre-trained
latent action model, which

803
00:40:51,970 --> 00:40:54,710
is what you use the
low-fidelity simulation for it

804
00:40:54,710 --> 00:40:58,090
to come up with this
structured action space.

805
00:40:58,090 --> 00:41:02,950
And then if the action space
is low-dimensional enough,

806
00:41:02,950 --> 00:41:07,930
you can actually learn to
control the various reward

807
00:41:07,930 --> 00:41:10,250
factors, where to look,
where to move your hand,

808
00:41:10,250 --> 00:41:16,590
where to move the base by
exploiting that causal matrix

809
00:41:16,590 --> 00:41:24,390
from the previous slide that
allows you to change the various

810
00:41:24,390 --> 00:41:28,550
action factors to
get the various--

811
00:41:28,550 --> 00:41:32,190
to maximize the
various reward terms.

812
00:41:32,190 --> 00:41:33,850
And then here's a demonstration.

813
00:41:33,850 --> 00:41:36,910
So at the start of
training, a robot

814
00:41:36,910 --> 00:41:38,327
has no idea how
to solve its task,

815
00:41:38,327 --> 00:41:40,202
just like in the previous
video I showed you.

816
00:41:40,202 --> 00:41:42,010
So it's sort of moving
around randomly.

817
00:41:42,010 --> 00:41:46,870
So here's the first minute or
so of training minute or two.

818
00:41:46,870 --> 00:41:51,510
But after only 40 minutes,
so it gets a behavior now

819
00:41:51,510 --> 00:41:55,590
where we've given
it reward for being

820
00:41:55,590 --> 00:42:00,890
able to successfully wipe off
the blue marks from the board.

821
00:42:00,890 --> 00:42:04,750
And it's learned to successfully
move the base, the camera,

822
00:42:04,750 --> 00:42:09,900
and the arm in unison to be
able to achieve that task.

823
00:42:09,900 --> 00:42:11,700
And again, with
all of the starting

824
00:42:11,700 --> 00:42:15,100
from random behavior and 40
minutes later having learned

825
00:42:15,100 --> 00:42:15,920
this skill.

826
00:42:15,920 --> 00:42:18,980
But by leveraging this
very efficient action space

827
00:42:18,980 --> 00:42:20,380
that was learned in simulation.

828
00:42:20,380 --> 00:42:22,640
Using exactly the
same methodology,

829
00:42:22,640 --> 00:42:24,920
we can end that same
latent action space.

830
00:42:24,920 --> 00:42:30,100
It can learn to sweep garbage
onto a tray or garbage

831
00:42:30,100 --> 00:42:33,220
into a bag.

832
00:42:33,220 --> 00:42:35,500
And also it can learn
to wipe the whiteboard

833
00:42:35,500 --> 00:42:37,427
with or without an
obstacle in front of it.

834
00:42:37,427 --> 00:42:39,260
And so we've done these
four different tasks

835
00:42:39,260 --> 00:42:41,180
that I just mentioned.

836
00:42:41,180 --> 00:42:45,900
And compared to doing all of
the learning in simulation

837
00:42:45,900 --> 00:42:52,260
or a couple of existing
methods, one that which

838
00:42:52,260 --> 00:42:54,380
learns entirely in the
low-level action space

839
00:42:54,380 --> 00:42:57,420
rather than in this more
factorized abstract action

840
00:42:57,420 --> 00:42:58,980
space.

841
00:42:58,980 --> 00:43:02,120
Also another method called RLPD.

842
00:43:02,120 --> 00:43:03,780
And we both have
higher success rate

843
00:43:03,780 --> 00:43:06,160
at those four tasks,
and importantly,

844
00:43:06,160 --> 00:43:10,160
fewer safety violations
during learning

845
00:43:10,160 --> 00:43:13,480
so that the robot can actually
do this learning efficiently

846
00:43:13,480 --> 00:43:14,580
in the real world.

847
00:43:14,580 --> 00:43:17,160
And in all cases, all of
those policies trained

848
00:43:17,160 --> 00:43:19,440
in less than one hour
on the real robots

849
00:43:19,440 --> 00:43:21,360
so that we end up with
an autonomous agent

850
00:43:21,360 --> 00:43:24,880
that's able to
learn in that case.

851
00:43:24,880 --> 00:43:28,130
So you can learn a lot more
details from the paper.

852
00:43:28,130 --> 00:43:30,720

853
00:43:30,720 --> 00:43:35,120
And this is a theme for a lot
of the recent research in my lab

854
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:38,920
is trying to use
simulation, but to allow

855
00:43:38,920 --> 00:43:44,000
for learning in the real
world, but not necessarily

856
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:46,320
by just learning
all in simulation

857
00:43:46,320 --> 00:43:48,180
and transferring
the policy directly,

858
00:43:48,180 --> 00:43:50,880
but instead leveraging the
simulation in more creative ways

859
00:43:50,880 --> 00:43:56,260
like here to pre-train an
efficient action space.

860
00:43:56,260 --> 00:43:57,760
And of course, like
many, we've also

861
00:43:57,760 --> 00:44:00,620
been exploring how can
we use foundation models,

862
00:44:00,620 --> 00:44:04,092
large language models and
visual language models

863
00:44:04,092 --> 00:44:08,280
to not necessarily control the
low-level actions of the robot,

864
00:44:08,280 --> 00:44:10,540
but to provide some
of the common sense

865
00:44:10,540 --> 00:44:12,430
knowledge for the robot.

866
00:44:12,430 --> 00:44:15,940

867
00:44:15,940 --> 00:44:19,780
So that's what I wanted to do
in terms of the embodiment, sort

868
00:44:19,780 --> 00:44:23,420
of a somewhat deep dive
into a particular research

869
00:44:23,420 --> 00:44:26,940
paper on embodiment.

870
00:44:26,940 --> 00:44:33,420
I think I have time to also go
in a little bit of a deep dive

871
00:44:33,420 --> 00:44:34,840
on GT Sophy as well.

872
00:44:34,840 --> 00:44:38,140
So I've given you
an overview of this.

873
00:44:38,140 --> 00:44:39,160
Oops, where is it?

874
00:44:39,160 --> 00:44:43,420

875
00:44:43,420 --> 00:44:44,720
Oh, I think I have it here.

