892
00:45:34,292 --> 00:45:36,750
There was a lot of data that
went in, a lot of engineering.

893
00:45:36,750 --> 00:45:41,610
It wasn't just sort of a new
out-of-the-blue principle.

894
00:45:41,610 --> 00:45:45,610
So there's a lot of data
and top-notch engineering

895
00:45:45,610 --> 00:45:46,550
and compute.

896
00:45:46,550 --> 00:45:49,690
But there also were a few
algorithmic and methodological

897
00:45:49,690 --> 00:45:51,510
insights that went into
making it possible.

898
00:45:51,510 --> 00:45:53,677
And I'll try to give you a
sense of those right now.

899
00:45:53,677 --> 00:45:57,770
Those are what are
featured in the article.

900
00:45:57,770 --> 00:46:00,842
Just as a reminder, in
every video, I show you,

901
00:46:00,842 --> 00:46:04,830
the colored cars are
going to be GT Sophy

902
00:46:04,830 --> 00:46:07,550
and the white ones
are controlled

903
00:46:07,550 --> 00:46:12,270
by one of the very best
drivers at GT Sophy.

904
00:46:12,270 --> 00:46:16,310
I've already told you
about the simulator itself

905
00:46:16,310 --> 00:46:20,710
and that we do this with
reinforcement learning.

906
00:46:20,710 --> 00:46:23,950
The main challenges
that made this

907
00:46:23,950 --> 00:46:27,630
more difficult
than previous tasks

908
00:46:27,630 --> 00:46:29,950
was that it's real-time control.

909
00:46:29,950 --> 00:46:34,343
So we had to come up with
actions at 10 times per second,

910
00:46:34,343 --> 00:46:36,510
where it's acquiring a deep
understanding of the car

911
00:46:36,510 --> 00:46:38,650
dynamics, "understanding"
in quotes,

912
00:46:38,650 --> 00:46:43,350
but the ability to control a
car following racing lines,

913
00:46:43,350 --> 00:46:45,870
precision racing maneuvers.

914
00:46:45,870 --> 00:46:49,190
And when I say at the edge,
it means, if the car has--

915
00:46:49,190 --> 00:46:52,070
the controller has to
figure out if it goes--

916
00:46:52,070 --> 00:46:53,470
how fast it can go.

917
00:46:53,470 --> 00:46:55,750
If it goes any faster,
it's going to spin out.

918
00:46:55,750 --> 00:46:57,990
But if it goes any
slower, it's going

919
00:46:57,990 --> 00:46:59,330
to be easy for a human to pass.

920
00:46:59,330 --> 00:47:02,410
It's got to be right at that
edge of staying in control.

921
00:47:02,410 --> 00:47:04,210
There's also tactics,
like I showed you

922
00:47:04,210 --> 00:47:06,230
already the example
of defensive moves,

923
00:47:06,230 --> 00:47:08,870
but there's many more
that I'll show you about.

924
00:47:08,870 --> 00:47:12,010
And also there's this really
interesting and ill-defined

925
00:47:12,010 --> 00:47:15,570
aspect of etiquette,
where you can't just

926
00:47:15,570 --> 00:47:17,330
push another car off the track.

927
00:47:17,330 --> 00:47:19,370
There's a human judge
that decides what's

928
00:47:19,370 --> 00:47:21,890
sportsmanlike and
not, and the agent

929
00:47:21,890 --> 00:47:26,050
has to have that
encoded as well.

930
00:47:26,050 --> 00:47:30,570
So in this case, the
actions that the car used

931
00:47:30,570 --> 00:47:33,230
were at 10 Hertz.

932
00:47:33,230 --> 00:47:35,810
It could either control
the brake or the throttle

933
00:47:35,810 --> 00:47:38,210
to be full backwards
or full forwards, can

934
00:47:38,210 --> 00:47:39,790
turn full left or full right.

935
00:47:39,790 --> 00:47:44,133
It's really already a factored
low-dimensional action space.

936
00:47:44,133 --> 00:47:45,550
There's just two
dimensions to it.

937
00:47:45,550 --> 00:47:48,050
So we don't have to go through
what we did with SLAC to try

938
00:47:48,050 --> 00:47:49,690
to discover that.

939
00:47:49,690 --> 00:47:52,150
The agent already has
this natural factored,

940
00:47:52,150 --> 00:47:53,930
low-dimensional action space.

941
00:47:53,930 --> 00:47:56,010
The rewards initially
are just how fast

942
00:47:56,010 --> 00:47:57,410
it moves along the track.

943
00:47:57,410 --> 00:48:00,850
And then we added that's mainly
the positive rewards and then

944
00:48:00,850 --> 00:48:04,120
also penalties for the tire
slipping or going off the track

945
00:48:04,120 --> 00:48:05,880
or hitting a wall.

946
00:48:05,880 --> 00:48:08,600
And then there were initially
in the initial work,

947
00:48:08,600 --> 00:48:12,400
we had not visual features.

948
00:48:12,400 --> 00:48:14,880
We have a couple of
papers since then

949
00:48:14,880 --> 00:48:19,260
where we have used visual
inputs to help the car control.

950
00:48:19,260 --> 00:48:20,720
But in the initial
paper, we were

951
00:48:20,720 --> 00:48:22,660
giving things like
the car's velocity,

952
00:48:22,660 --> 00:48:24,120
acceleration, roll,
pitch, and yaw

953
00:48:24,120 --> 00:48:28,740
load on the tires and position
along the track to help it.

954
00:48:28,740 --> 00:48:35,060

955
00:48:35,060 --> 00:48:37,760
The compute infrastructure
was really important.

956
00:48:37,760 --> 00:48:42,380
But by doing this within
the Sony ecosystem,

957
00:48:42,380 --> 00:48:46,080
we were able to partner with
Polyphony Digital and Sony

958
00:48:46,080 --> 00:48:48,720
Interactive Entertainment
for the PlayStation group

959
00:48:48,720 --> 00:48:52,233
to have access to a large
bank of PlayStations,

960
00:48:52,233 --> 00:48:54,400
which we could then use in
a sort of an Actor-Critic

961
00:48:54,400 --> 00:48:59,860
kind of scenario where we have
a lot of roll-up workers getting

962
00:48:59,860 --> 00:49:01,740
data on what happens
when you take an action

963
00:49:01,740 --> 00:49:03,740
and what state comes back.

964
00:49:03,740 --> 00:49:05,720
And then we used--

965
00:49:05,720 --> 00:49:08,900
and we store that data
in experience replay

966
00:49:08,900 --> 00:49:12,780
buffer and then use an
Actor-Critic method based

967
00:49:12,780 --> 00:49:16,180
on Soft Actor-Critic,
but introduced

968
00:49:16,180 --> 00:49:19,060
a new algorithm called
quantile-regression, or QR-SAC,

969
00:49:19,060 --> 00:49:21,860
Quantile-Regression
Soft Actor-Critic, to be

970
00:49:21,860 --> 00:49:25,260
able to train the policy, which
is pi, that's what actions you

971
00:49:25,260 --> 00:49:27,940
take, and the critic, which
is the value function, which

972
00:49:27,940 --> 00:49:31,020
is basically an
estimate of how good it

973
00:49:31,020 --> 00:49:35,420
is to be where you currently
are in the state that you're in.

974
00:49:35,420 --> 00:49:38,180
And so here's a slide
of technical details

975
00:49:38,180 --> 00:49:40,640
for people who are familiar
with reinforcement learning.

976
00:49:40,640 --> 00:49:43,200
Don't worry if this
doesn't all speak to you.

977
00:49:43,200 --> 00:49:50,940
But basically, QR-SAC was
learning, not just the average

978
00:49:50,940 --> 00:49:53,080
or the mean value
of taking an action,

979
00:49:53,080 --> 00:49:54,280
but the full distribution.

980
00:49:54,280 --> 00:49:56,500
What's the likelihood of a
really bad thing happening

981
00:49:56,500 --> 00:49:58,140
versus a really
good thing happening

982
00:49:58,140 --> 00:50:00,360
when you take an action.

983
00:50:00,360 --> 00:50:04,800
So it's learning a quantile or a
distributional RL value function

984
00:50:04,800 --> 00:50:08,080
as it's called,
and was also using

985
00:50:08,080 --> 00:50:10,440
N-step returns rather
than one-step returns

986
00:50:10,440 --> 00:50:11,820
to improve its performance.

987
00:50:11,820 --> 00:50:13,280
The numbers here
on the right are

988
00:50:13,280 --> 00:50:15,400
the lap time in a
particular configuration

989
00:50:15,400 --> 00:50:17,800
on a particular
track, and so smaller

990
00:50:17,800 --> 00:50:20,780
is better if you can get around
the track in 114 seconds.

991
00:50:20,780 --> 00:50:23,500
That's better than 115 seconds.

992
00:50:23,500 --> 00:50:25,780
So it's about almost
one second improvement,

993
00:50:25,780 --> 00:50:28,920
which is pretty huge in this
domain between the vanilla Soft

994
00:50:28,920 --> 00:50:31,000
Actor-Critic and
our new algorithm.

995
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:35,120
And we also found the sweet
spot for how the N-step returns.

996
00:50:35,120 --> 00:50:36,600
The details are in
the paper, and I

997
00:50:36,600 --> 00:50:40,480
have talks that go into much
more detail on how QR-SAC works

998
00:50:40,480 --> 00:50:42,500
and the neural net
training details.

999
00:50:42,500 --> 00:50:48,400
But the bottom line
is that after an hour,

1000
00:50:48,400 --> 00:50:51,560
the car is able to move
forwards, not run into walls

1001
00:50:51,560 --> 00:50:54,040
or drive off the track,
but not very efficiently.

1002
00:50:54,040 --> 00:50:57,350
After four hours,
to move much more

1003
00:50:57,350 --> 00:51:01,170
smoothly, after eight
hours, pretty expertly.

1004
00:51:01,170 --> 00:51:03,350
And actually after a day,
although we can run it

1005
00:51:03,350 --> 00:51:05,770
for a week, it's really
at superhuman performance,

1006
00:51:05,770 --> 00:51:07,090
like a person can't control.

1007
00:51:07,090 --> 00:51:10,210
And it's hard to see, I
know, on online in Zoom,

1008
00:51:10,210 --> 00:51:14,070
but it's cutting the
corners just exactly right

1009
00:51:14,070 --> 00:51:18,510
in the optimal way and in
a way that a person can't

1010
00:51:18,510 --> 00:51:19,725
do any better.

1011
00:51:19,725 --> 00:51:21,350
And in fact, it does
better than almost

1012
00:51:21,350 --> 00:51:22,770
every person in the world.

1013
00:51:22,770 --> 00:51:26,870
We tracked it against after 4
hours, 8 hours, and 24 hours.

1014
00:51:26,870 --> 00:51:31,550
The blue here is a histogram of
the best 100-lap times reported

1015
00:51:31,550 --> 00:51:34,750
by people on a worldwide server.

1016
00:51:34,750 --> 00:51:38,670
And after 24 hours,
it's better than almost

1017
00:51:38,670 --> 00:51:41,290
all of the reported lap times.

1018
00:51:41,290 --> 00:51:44,790
And if you take the
average of a person,

1019
00:51:44,790 --> 00:51:46,750
the best person's
average is shown here

1020
00:51:46,750 --> 00:51:50,370
on the left and the distribution
of GT Sophy's times,

1021
00:51:50,370 --> 00:51:52,550
better than the second,
third, fourth, and fifth

1022
00:51:52,550 --> 00:51:54,790
and on par with the best
person in the world.

1023
00:51:54,790 --> 00:51:56,403
And that's just
on this one track.

1024
00:51:56,403 --> 00:51:58,570
On the other two tracks,
that's significantly better

1025
00:51:58,570 --> 00:52:02,250
than the best
person's average time.

1026
00:52:02,250 --> 00:52:05,950
And so that was the first step.

1027
00:52:05,950 --> 00:52:08,850
But in this domain, being
faster wasn't enough.

1028
00:52:08,850 --> 00:52:12,050
There were two other
challenges to solve.

1029
00:52:12,050 --> 00:52:15,090
Like I said, there's the race
car control, which I showed you

1030
00:52:15,090 --> 00:52:17,950
we could do, but there's also
the tactics and the etiquette.

1031
00:52:17,950 --> 00:52:19,530
So to be able to do--

1032
00:52:19,530 --> 00:52:21,610
to bring that, we
augmented the state space

1033
00:52:21,610 --> 00:52:24,610
to include knowledge of where
the other cars are nearby,

1034
00:52:24,610 --> 00:52:26,310
their relative
position, velocity,

1035
00:52:26,310 --> 00:52:29,210
and acceleration for the cars
in front and beyond and behind.

1036
00:52:29,210 --> 00:52:31,290
Also a reward for
passing, a penalty

1037
00:52:31,290 --> 00:52:33,400
for being passed
that encourages cars

1038
00:52:33,400 --> 00:52:35,650
to be a little more risky
when they're near other cars

1039
00:52:35,650 --> 00:52:37,530
to get around them.

1040
00:52:37,530 --> 00:52:40,550
And then importantly, we
had to curate the data.

1041
00:52:40,550 --> 00:52:44,510
This was another
one of innovations

1042
00:52:44,510 --> 00:52:47,550
so that the car gets the
experience that it needs.

1043
00:52:47,550 --> 00:52:50,410
So we put a bunch of
scenarios where it's just

1044
00:52:50,410 --> 00:52:51,670
racing on the empty track.

1045
00:52:51,670 --> 00:52:53,030
We can put 20 cars--

1046
00:52:53,030 --> 00:52:54,447
the important thing
to know is you

1047
00:52:54,447 --> 00:52:56,470
can put 20 cars on the
track at any one time

1048
00:52:56,470 --> 00:52:58,750
on a place in the
game, and so we

1049
00:52:58,750 --> 00:53:01,890
could put just 20 cars, spread
out around to get a lot of data.

1050
00:53:01,890 --> 00:53:03,630
But then we also
created some situations

1051
00:53:03,630 --> 00:53:05,950
where there's one car
behind one other car,

1052
00:53:05,950 --> 00:53:08,070
or one behind two or
one behind three or one

1053
00:53:08,070 --> 00:53:12,510
behind seven, so it gets
experience driving in traffic.

1054
00:53:12,510 --> 00:53:15,590
And in particular,
it gets experience

1055
00:53:15,590 --> 00:53:18,310
where it starts behind
another car on a straightaway

1056
00:53:18,310 --> 00:53:20,110
where it can--
it's encouraged to

1057
00:53:20,110 --> 00:53:23,110
but not explicitly
told to learn what's

1058
00:53:23,110 --> 00:53:24,883
known as a slipstream
pass, where it just

1059
00:53:24,883 --> 00:53:26,050
sort of like bicycle racing.

1060
00:53:26,050 --> 00:53:28,870
You draft behind the other
car so that you have lower air

1061
00:53:28,870 --> 00:53:30,870
resistance, and you can
get a lot of momentum

1062
00:53:30,870 --> 00:53:35,190
and then bounce out from behind
them and be able to pass them.

1063
00:53:35,190 --> 00:53:37,270
To be able to learn
that, it's important

1064
00:53:37,270 --> 00:53:39,810
that the simulator actually
models this slipstream effect

1065
00:53:39,810 --> 00:53:41,270
and it does.

1066
00:53:41,270 --> 00:53:44,850
And then also, where you start
behind another car in a corner,

1067
00:53:44,850 --> 00:53:46,350
so you learn these
sort of crossover

1068
00:53:46,350 --> 00:53:49,870
pass maneuvers, and also
a bunch of other tactics,

1069
00:53:49,870 --> 00:53:53,070
like what happens when you're in
a grid of the start of the car,

1070
00:53:53,070 --> 00:53:55,770
or if you've got this
particularly difficult parts

1071
00:53:55,770 --> 00:53:57,410
of the track.

1072
00:53:57,410 --> 00:54:00,570
And so one of the things
that was a key to the success

1073
00:54:00,570 --> 00:54:05,530
here was creating a carefully
engineered mix of data

1074
00:54:05,530 --> 00:54:09,730
in the experience replay
buffer so that various points

1075
00:54:09,730 --> 00:54:11,610
along the track, it
would be learning

1076
00:54:11,610 --> 00:54:16,050
from data from different
places, and then

1077
00:54:16,050 --> 00:54:18,370
create a mini batch for
training that gives the car

1078
00:54:18,370 --> 00:54:20,150
the right mix of experience.

1079
00:54:20,150 --> 00:54:22,730
And for the Nature paper, we
did this manually, but then

1080
00:54:22,730 --> 00:54:25,090
since then, we have worked
on automated methods

1081
00:54:25,090 --> 00:54:28,810
for coming up with the right
mix of data in this experience

1082
00:54:28,810 --> 00:54:30,450
replay buffer.

1083
00:54:30,450 --> 00:54:35,010
So some of the skills that
were learned by this agent

1084
00:54:35,010 --> 00:54:37,370
were things like that
slipstream pass where--

1085
00:54:37,370 --> 00:54:38,430
and these are narrated.

1086
00:54:38,430 --> 00:54:39,930
You can find some
talks online where

1087
00:54:39,930 --> 00:54:41,470
you hear the sound as well.

1088
00:54:41,470 --> 00:54:43,570
I don't have that
working right now,

1089
00:54:43,570 --> 00:54:46,270
but where the GT Sophy
is the colored car again.

1090
00:54:46,270 --> 00:54:49,850
It starts behind the other car,
gets in that low air resistance

1091
00:54:49,850 --> 00:54:53,920
mode, or your location
and is able to pass.

1092
00:54:53,920 --> 00:54:57,560
So that's an example
of that happening.

1093
00:54:57,560 --> 00:55:01,080
I already showed you
the defensive skill.

1094
00:55:01,080 --> 00:55:04,480
And here's a really
that double crossover,

1095
00:55:04,480 --> 00:55:09,920
where you're going to see
the two different cars--

1096
00:55:09,920 --> 00:55:12,960
the green and the gray,
I think, start out

1097
00:55:12,960 --> 00:55:16,000
behind the two human drivers.

1098
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:19,720
But by the end of this turn,
they come out, both of them

1099
00:55:19,720 --> 00:55:21,500
in front of the human drivers.

1100
00:55:21,500 --> 00:55:23,840
And so you see on the right
the position or the line

1101
00:55:23,840 --> 00:55:26,840
that they took to be able to
successfully do that crossover

1102
00:55:26,840 --> 00:55:27,340
pass.

1103
00:55:27,340 --> 00:55:30,360
And again, this was not
programmed in or explicitly

1104
00:55:30,360 --> 00:55:31,620
rewarded in any way.

1105
00:55:31,620 --> 00:55:34,520
It was just part of
the overall learning

1106
00:55:34,520 --> 00:55:37,060
process and the experience
that we gave them.

1107
00:55:37,060 --> 00:55:42,040
And then finally, there was
this aspect of etiquette.

1108
00:55:42,040 --> 00:55:44,900
I'm not going to go into
the details either here,

1109
00:55:44,900 --> 00:55:51,520
but there's things like trying
to avoid avoidable collisions,

1110
00:55:51,520 --> 00:55:54,580
forcing a driver off the track,
doing things illegitimately

1111
00:55:54,580 --> 00:55:56,360
that are all sort
of judgment words.

1112
00:55:56,360 --> 00:55:59,020
Things that a
human Steward would

1113
00:55:59,020 --> 00:56:02,860
have to assess whether
they're happening or not.

1114
00:56:02,860 --> 00:56:05,362
But if you don't do
this, you'll end up

1115
00:56:05,362 --> 00:56:07,320
with aggressive agents
like the one shown here.

1116
00:56:07,320 --> 00:56:08,695
Here's also showing
the interface

1117
00:56:08,695 --> 00:56:10,052
of a person controlling the car.

1118
00:56:10,052 --> 00:56:11,760
But here's what you're
not allowed to do.

1119
00:56:11,760 --> 00:56:14,540
You can't just push a car
off the track like that.

1120
00:56:14,540 --> 00:56:16,240
But you also don't
want to be too timid.

1121
00:56:16,240 --> 00:56:19,100
So this was a clip where
GT Sophy was in front

1122
00:56:19,100 --> 00:56:21,903
and the human
announcer was saying,

1123
00:56:21,903 --> 00:56:24,320
there's no way that the person's
going to be able to pass.

1124
00:56:24,320 --> 00:56:25,560
It's a commanding lead.

1125
00:56:25,560 --> 00:56:27,700
But then GT Sophy
actually hit the brakes

1126
00:56:27,700 --> 00:56:29,300
because it was too
timid, it was too

1127
00:56:29,300 --> 00:56:31,020
concerned about
causing a collision,

1128
00:56:31,020 --> 00:56:33,920
and allowed the person to pass.

1129
00:56:33,920 --> 00:56:38,100
And so we needed to come up
with ways of mitigating that.

1130
00:56:38,100 --> 00:56:41,820
I think the human pass is
coming up right around here.

1131
00:56:41,820 --> 00:56:46,260
Right now, GT Sophy still in the
front, but I think you'll see--

1132
00:56:46,260 --> 00:56:50,080
yeah, here's Tomoaki
Yamanaka, the human expert,

1133
00:56:50,080 --> 00:56:56,560
now he has finally passed GT
Sophy because of that timidity.

1134
00:56:56,560 --> 00:56:59,280
So we added a bunch
of reward signals

1135
00:56:59,280 --> 00:57:02,880
to make sure the agent
wouldn't be too--

1136
00:57:02,880 --> 00:57:05,920

1137
00:57:05,920 --> 00:57:09,920
wouldn't get into
collisions and also

1138
00:57:09,920 --> 00:57:12,000
especially wouldn't
cause collisions

1139
00:57:12,000 --> 00:57:15,380
that it would be judged as being
at fault for and penalized for.

1140
00:57:15,380 --> 00:57:17,040
And so we did an
analysis in the paper

1141
00:57:17,040 --> 00:57:21,960
of how the different mix
of opponents and the reward

1142
00:57:21,960 --> 00:57:24,600
functions would lead
to different aspects

1143
00:57:24,600 --> 00:57:27,088
or different agents.

1144
00:57:27,088 --> 00:57:28,880
I'm running out of
time, so I'm not going--

1145
00:57:28,880 --> 00:57:31,130
I want to leave time for
plenty of time for questions.

1146
00:57:31,130 --> 00:57:33,620
So there's also an
important aspect here of,

1147
00:57:33,620 --> 00:57:35,140
we trained lots of
different agents,

1148
00:57:35,140 --> 00:57:36,848
but how do we choose
the one that's going

1149
00:57:36,848 --> 00:57:39,340
to go into the actual race.

1150
00:57:39,340 --> 00:57:42,200
That's an interesting aspect
that's covered in the paper.

1151
00:57:42,200 --> 00:57:45,640
But the bottom line is
that in July, the humans

1152
00:57:45,640 --> 00:57:50,270
outraced the people and
so we had a rematch.

1153
00:57:50,270 --> 00:57:53,610
And we analyzed what went wrong.

1154
00:57:53,610 --> 00:57:56,510
We spent a few
months improving it.

1155
00:57:56,510 --> 00:58:04,830
And by October created
a set of agents

1156
00:58:04,830 --> 00:58:07,270
that was able to finish
first and second place

1157
00:58:07,270 --> 00:58:10,270
in an eight-car race
for GT Sophy cars

1158
00:58:10,270 --> 00:58:13,750
against four humans in a
setting where you got points

1159
00:58:13,750 --> 00:58:18,190
based on 10 points for finishing
first, eight for second,

1160
00:58:18,190 --> 00:58:21,310
and then I think 7, 6
5, 4, 3 for the rest,

1161
00:58:21,310 --> 00:58:22,757
or something like that.

1162
00:58:22,757 --> 00:58:25,340
That was on the previous slide,
I think, what the scoring was.

1163
00:58:25,340 --> 00:58:29,150

1164
00:58:29,150 --> 00:58:32,210
Yeah, 10, 8, and 6 for the
first three places and then 5,

1165
00:58:32,210 --> 00:58:36,750
4, 3, 2, 1 for the other places,
and double points for the harder

1166
00:58:36,750 --> 00:58:38,390
start task.

1167
00:58:38,390 --> 00:58:40,315
But the bottom
line is by October,

1168
00:58:40,315 --> 00:58:42,690
we were able to finish first
and second in all the races.

1169
00:58:42,690 --> 00:58:45,576
And interestingly,
there were some quotes

1170
00:58:45,576 --> 00:58:48,730
from some of the drivers,
including Emily Jones.

1171
00:58:48,730 --> 00:58:50,150
I'm not going to play--

1172
00:58:50,150 --> 00:58:50,870
that's her there.

1173
00:58:50,870 --> 00:58:53,836
But saying things like,
I've learned something

1174
00:58:53,836 --> 00:58:55,530
from the agents.

1175
00:58:55,530 --> 00:58:58,250
I watched them, they're driving
differently than I would

1176
00:58:58,250 --> 00:58:59,610
and they've improved.

1177
00:58:59,610 --> 00:59:01,970
There are things
that I ought to try.

1178
00:59:01,970 --> 00:59:04,890
And now, like I
said earlier, this

1179
00:59:04,890 --> 00:59:09,410
has been permanently deployed
in the game in GT Sophy,

1180
00:59:09,410 --> 00:59:12,730
and there's been a lot of
testimonials from gamers

1181
00:59:12,730 --> 00:59:18,490
that this is a real big
addition to Gran Turismo,

1182
00:59:18,490 --> 00:59:24,130
and now it's out there on
many more cars and tracks.

1183
00:59:24,130 --> 00:59:25,763
But there's still a lot to do.

1184
00:59:25,763 --> 00:59:27,930
One of the things that we're
trying to get the agent

1185
00:59:27,930 --> 00:59:28,870
to drive with style.

1186
00:59:28,870 --> 00:59:33,170
So here's if we added a reward
for drifting the car, learned

1187
00:59:33,170 --> 00:59:36,070
to do something like
this to go around turns,

1188
00:59:36,070 --> 00:59:39,690
which is not something
any human driver would do,

1189
00:59:39,690 --> 00:59:42,290
but it sort of does
a little spin-move,

1190
00:59:42,290 --> 00:59:47,420
moving into a turn and
optimally comes out of it,

1191
00:59:47,420 --> 00:59:51,638
and also trying to
make it more fun.

1192
00:59:51,638 --> 00:59:53,930
That was an important part
of putting it into the game.

1193
00:59:53,930 --> 00:59:55,430
It wouldn't be fun
if you could just

1194
00:59:55,430 --> 00:59:59,710
race against an agent that can
outrace all of the best people.

1195
00:59:59,710 --> 01:00:01,210
At least if I were
playing the game,

1196
01:00:01,210 --> 01:00:04,090
I would see the agent just for
the first couple of seconds,

1197
01:00:04,090 --> 01:00:06,250
and then it would be gone
ahead of me for the rest.

1198
01:00:06,250 --> 01:00:09,630
So we needed to have a way to
make it also adjustable in terms

1199
01:00:09,630 --> 01:00:10,950
of its skill level.

1200
01:00:10,950 --> 01:00:17,070
So again, this was work of a
group of people at Sony AI.

1201
01:00:17,070 --> 01:00:20,430
And so I encourage you,
there are hour long talks

1202
01:00:20,430 --> 01:00:22,630
about this on my web page.

1203
01:00:22,630 --> 01:00:26,070
You can get the paper as well.

1204
01:00:26,070 --> 01:00:31,250
But that's mainly what I
wanted to show you today.

1205
01:00:31,250 --> 01:00:35,070
I know it's been a little
bit of a whirlwind.

1206
01:00:35,070 --> 01:00:36,150
But I wanted to--

1207
01:00:36,150 --> 01:00:38,110
one of the main things
I wanted to drive home

1208
01:00:38,110 --> 01:00:40,670
is that autonomous agents
research has been around

1209
01:00:40,670 --> 01:00:41,830
for a long time.

1210
01:00:41,830 --> 01:00:49,340
Agents are quite a general
thing in intelligent systems

1211
01:00:49,340 --> 01:00:52,100
that interact with the
environment through sensors.

1212
01:00:52,100 --> 01:00:55,580
Sorry, this is
getting gotten jumpy--

1213
01:00:55,580 --> 01:01:00,180
there we go-- through
sensors and effectors.

1214
01:01:00,180 --> 01:01:02,620
And then an intelligent,
complete agent

1215
01:01:02,620 --> 01:01:05,300
is one that has perception,
cognition, and action

1216
01:01:05,300 --> 01:01:09,100
from those sensors in between
the sensors and the actuators.

1217
